     "type": "application",
     "title": "Audit Your Own Contract Template",
     "content": "Pull up the contract or statement of work you currently use—or, if you don't have one, the last email where you agreed to freelance terms with a client. Run it through this five-point audit.\n\nFirst, scope: does it list specific deliverables and a specific number of revision rounds, or does it use vague verbs like 'help with' or 'support'? If vague, rewrite it as a numbered list.\n\nSecond, kill fee: if this client vanished tomorrow, does your agreement say what percentage of the fee you'd keep for work already done? If not, draft a three-tier schedule and add it.\n\nThird, payment terms: does your agreement state an exact number of days until payment is due, and a specific late penalty, not just 'we'll follow up'? If it just says 'net 30' with no penalty, add an interest clause.\n\nFourth, IP: does it say explicitly when ownership or license transfers to the client—on signing, on delivery, or on final payment? Tie it to full payment if you can, since that gives you leverage.\n\nFifth, indemnification: does it protect you from claims caused by material the client supplied that you didn't create? If this section doesn't exist at all, that is your highest-priority gap to fix before your next contract goes out.\n\nRewrite one contract this week using all five fixes, and treat it as your new default template for every client going forward.",
